  <span class="mono-label">RING 1 · CORNERSTONE</span>
  <h1>How does a virtual job simulation compare to a real internship?</h1>
  <p class="answer-capsule">A simulation gives you a focused task and a fast deliverable. An internship gives you context and relationships. The strongest students use simulations to build proof before they apply for internships, then use the internship to deepen their signal.</p>

  <div class="tldr">
    <ul>
      <li>An internship gives you company context. A simulation gives you a fast deliverable.</li>
      <li>Internships last 8 to 12 weeks. Simulations last 3 to 7 days.</li>
      <li>The best strategy: simulation first for proof, internship second for depth.</li>
      <li>A scored receipt is more evaluable than a reference letter.</li>
      <li>zero tasks are real company operations, not generic exercises.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>


  <section>
    <h2>What does a real internship give you that a simulation cannot?</h2>
    <p>A real internship gives you context: meetings, politics, deadlines set by real managers, feedback from colleagues who will be in your industry for years. You learn how work actually flows inside a company. A simulation gives you a task. An internship gives you an environment.</p>
    <p>The trade-off is time and access. An internship lasts 8 to 12 weeks and requires an application process you may not survive. A simulation lasts 3 to 7 days and is open to anyone.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What does a simulation do better than an internship?</h2>
    <p>A simulation is faster, more focused, and gives you a deliverable you can show immediately. An internship may bury you in admin work. A simulation forces you to produce one clear output. If you choose the right simulation, that output becomes a portfolio piece.</p>
    <p>The key difference is evaluation. Most internships end with a reference letter or a line on a resume. A well-built simulation ends with a scored receipt that shows exactly how your work compares to professionals.</p>
  </section>
  <p>See also: <a href="/learn/forage-alternative-for-students">related guide</a> and <a href="/learn/what-should-i-do-this-summer-if-i-didnt-get-an-internship">companion article</a>.</p>

  <section>
    <h2>How do you combine both for maximum signal?</h2>
    <p>Use the simulation to build proof before you apply for internships. Submit the receipt with your application. Recruiters now have something to evaluate before they decide to interview you. Once you are inside the internship, use the same rubric discipline to extract one portfolio-quality deliverable.</p>
    <p>See <a href="/how-it-works">how zero works</a> or compare <a href="/vs-forage">zero vs. Forage</a> for the difference between a certificate and a receipt.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What did the pilot reveal about simulations vs. real work?</h2>
    <p>In a Middle East university pilot, Spring 2026, students who built receipts inside zero's simulator returned to the platform specifically to read feedback on their submissions. Generic career advice was ignored. Per-submission feedback drove every return visit. The most common insight: "I didn't know I could do this kind of work without a job."</p>
  </section>


